1
00:00:04,780 --> 00:00:11,780
In this video, we're going to use quantifix dip strips to test an organic solution for the presence of peroxides.

2
00:00:11,780 --> 00:00:21,780
This process should be done inside of a fume hood using the appropriate PPE, including safety glasses, lab coat, gloves, long pants, and closed-toed shoes.

3
00:00:21,780 --> 00:00:28,780
First, open the container and remove only as many test strips that are needed.

4
00:00:28,780 --> 00:00:33,780
Try to avoid touching the test field during handling as much as possible.

5
00:00:34,780 --> 00:00:37,780
Close the container immediately afterwards.

6
00:00:37,780 --> 00:00:41,780
We can dip the strip directly into the container because this is waste.

7
00:00:41,780 --> 00:00:50,780
If your test solution is not waste, you'll want to transfer a small amount to another container for testing to avoid potential contamination of the chemical.

8
00:00:50,780 --> 00:00:54,780
Shake off excess liquid and wait 5 seconds.

9
00:00:54,780 --> 00:01:01,780
Quantifix instructions specify adding a drop of water after dipping the test strip when testing organic solutions.

10
00:01:01,780 --> 00:01:05,780
This is a really important step which greatly affects the accuracy of the results.

11
00:01:05,780 --> 00:01:09,780
Notice how much the color changes after adding the drop of water.

12
00:01:09,780 --> 00:01:20,720
For consistency, we recommend adding this step when testing aqueous solutions as well, as it won't affect the results.

13
00:01:20,720 --> 00:01:23,720
Next, compare the test field with the color scale.

14
00:01:23,720 --> 00:01:27,720
If peroxides are present, the test field will turn blue.

15
00:01:27,720 --> 00:01:32,720
Record the peroxide level, date, and your initials on the PFC label.

16
00:01:32,720 --> 00:01:35,720
Discard and replace test strips when they expire.
